---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TopBar from '../../components/TopBar.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const publishedDate = '2026-09-07';
const articleTitle = "RENT vs BUY: When, Why, and How";
const articleDescription = "A plain-English guide for people wondering if they should keep renting or start buying in Toronto. Covers when it makes sense, why it matters, and how to actually get started — with a live calculator you can play with.";
const articleUrl = 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/rent-vs-buy';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: articleTitle,
  description: articleDescription,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Peter Law', url: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/about' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Peter Law — Toronto Real Estate',
    logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/favicon.svg' },
  },
  datePublished: publishedDate,
  dateModified: publishedDate,
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': articleUrl },
  keywords: 'rent vs buy Toronto, should I rent or buy, first time home buyer Toronto, rent or buy calculator, Toronto real estate tips, when to buy a house Ontario, how to buy a first home Canada, break-even years buying vs renting',
};

const hreflangAlternates = [
  { hreflang: 'en-CA', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/rent-vs-buy/' },
  { hreflang: 'x-default', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/rent-vs-buy/' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`${articleTitle} | Peter Law`}
  description={articleDescription}
  bodyClass="guide-page article-page"
  hreflangAlternates={hreflangAlternates}
>
  <TopBar />
  <Header active="blog" />

  <article>
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow"><a href="/blog" class="back-link"><svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" style="vertical-align:-1px;margin-right:5px;" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 7H1M6 12L1 7l5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Blog</a> · Buyer Basics</div>
        <h1>{articleTitle}</h1>
        <p class="article-lead">Should you keep renting, or start buying? Nobody can answer that in one line. But you can answer it for <em>yourself</em> in about ten minutes if you know what to look at. Here's the short version — plus a free calculator you can play with.</p>
        <div class="article-byline">
          <span>By <a href="/about">Peter Law</a></span>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <time datetime={publishedDate}>September 7, 2026</time>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span>6 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="major" style="padding-top:32px;">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="prose-grid">
          <aside class="prose-side">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              <li><a href="#quick">The 30-second answer</a></li>
              <li><a href="#when">WHEN to think about buying</a></li>
              <li><a href="#why">WHY it matters</a></li>
              <li><a href="#how">HOW to actually get started</a></li>
              <li><a href="#calc">Try the calculator</a></li>
              <li><a href="#tips">Tips before you commit</a></li>
              <li><a href="#mistakes">Common mistakes</a></li>
            </ul>
          </aside>

          <div class="prose-body">

            <p id="quick"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Buying tends to win if you'll stay put at least <strong>5 years</strong>, have steady income, and can put down at least <strong>5%</strong> plus about <strong>4% more</strong> for closing costs. Renting tends to win if you might move soon, your income is uncertain, or the deposit you'd need would wipe out your savings. Neither is "smarter" — they solve different problems.</p>

            <div class="callout"><strong>Who this article is for:</strong> renters in the Toronto area (or anywhere in Ontario, really) who keep hearing "you should buy" from family and friends and want to figure out — with actual numbers, not vibes — whether it's the right move for them right now.</div>

            <details class="b60-section-toggle" id="when">
              <summary class="b60-section-toggle-summary">WHEN it makes sense to buy</summary>
              <div class="b60-section-body">

            <p>Three simple checks. If you can say "yes" to all three, you're in the buying zone. If you say "no" to any, keep renting for now — and there's no shame in that.</p>

            <ul class="bullets">
              <li><strong>You'll stay put for at least 5 years.</strong> Buying a place, then selling it 2 years later, is expensive. Land Transfer Tax when you buy, real estate commission when you sell, legal fees both times. That stuff only pays off over time.</li>
              <li><strong>Your income is steady.</strong> Steady enough that a big monthly mortgage payment won't wreck you if your car breaks or you lose one client. Lenders will stress-test you at a higher rate before they'll approve a loan — you should stress-test yourself first.</li>
              <li><strong>You have the down payment PLUS about 4% for closing costs.</strong> A $500,000 place needs at least $25,000 down (5%). But you also need roughly $20,000 more for Land Transfer Tax, legal fees, inspection, and moving. Everyone forgets closing costs. Don't be that person.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="callout" style="border-left: 3px solid var(--ink); padding-left: 22px;">
              <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> if you're staying under 3 years, renting almost always wins. Between 3 and 5 years, it depends on your down payment, the market, and the interest rate. Over 5 years, buying usually pulls ahead — the longer you hold, the bigger the gap.
            </div>
              </div>
            </details>
<details class="b60-section-toggle" id="why">
  <summary class="b60-section-toggle-summary">WHY this matters (beyond the money)</summary>
  <div class="b60-section-body">

            <p>Money isn't the only thing on the table. There are three real trade-offs, and it helps to name them.</p>

            <h3>What you gain by buying</h3>
            <ul class="bullets">
              <li><strong>Equity.</strong> Every mortgage payment includes a bit of principal — that's you paying yourself. Over years, it adds up. That's why homeowners often walk away with a big cheque when they sell.</li>
              <li><strong>Price stability.</strong> Once you sign a mortgage, your payment stays about the same for the term (usually 5 years). Landlords can raise rent every year. In newer buildings (built after Nov 15, 2018), there's no cap.</li>
              <li><strong>Control.</strong> Paint the walls. Get a pet. Renovate. It's yours. That freedom is worth real money to some people.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>What you gain by renting</h3>
            <ul class="bullets">
              <li><strong>Flexibility.</strong> Job in Vancouver next year? Give 60 days notice and go. Homeowners can't move that fast — selling is slow and expensive.</li>
              <li><strong>No repair headache.</strong> Fridge dies? Roof leaks? Landlord's problem. Homeowners eat all of that themselves, plus their time to deal with it.</li>
              <li><strong>Liquid savings.</strong> The money you'd have locked up in a down payment stays in your account earning interest — or invested elsewhere. That flexibility has real value.</li>
            </ul>

            <p><strong>Renting is not "throwing money away."</strong> You're paying for a roof plus flexibility. Owners pay for a roof plus long-term equity. Different trades, different lives.</p>
  </div>
</details>
<details class="b60-section-toggle" id="how">
  <summary class="b60-section-toggle-summary">HOW to actually get started (if you're ready)</summary>
  <div class="b60-section-body">

            <p>If the three checks above landed as "yes," here's the order of operations. Do these in this sequence — skipping steps is where most first-time buyers get burned.</p>

            <ol class="bullets" style="list-style: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
              <li><strong>Pull your credit report.</strong> Free from <a href="https://borrowell.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Borrowell</a> or <a href="https://www.equifax.ca" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Equifax</a>. You want a score of at least <strong>680</strong> for the best mortgage rates. Under 600 and lenders get twitchy. Fix any errors before you shop.</li>
              <li><strong>Get pre-approved by a mortgage broker.</strong> Free. Takes about an hour. Tells you exactly what a lender will actually give you (not what a calculator says you could afford). Locks a rate for 90–120 days while you look. I can introduce you to brokers I've worked with.</li>
              <li><strong>Open a First Home Savings Account (FHSA).</strong> If you haven't already. Up to $8,000/year in tax-deductible contributions, tax-free withdrawal for your first home. <a href="/blog/fhsa">Full guide here.</a></li>
              <li><strong>Add up your closing costs.</strong> Land Transfer Tax (both provincial and Toronto if you're buying in the city), legal fees, home inspection, moving costs, and the first year of small repairs. See my <a href="/closing-costs">Closing Costs</a> page and <a href="/land-transfer-tax">Land Transfer Tax</a> calculator.</li>
              <li><strong>Look at the actual monthly cost.</strong> Not just the mortgage payment — add property tax, home insurance, condo fees if any, and heating/utilities. That's your real monthly commitment. My <a href="/payment-calculator">Mortgage Calculator</a> shows all three scenarios side-by-side.</li>
              <li><strong>Then, and only then, start shopping.</strong> Working with a REALTOR® is free for buyers (the seller pays commission). Their job is to represent your side — negotiate price, spot red flags, coordinate the inspection, and get you to the closing table.</li>
            </ol>
  </div>
</details>
<h2 id="calc">Try the calculator</h2>
            <p>Instead of guessing, plug in your own numbers. My <strong>Rent vs Buy Calculator</strong> compares the total monthly cost of buying vs renting, then shows the long-term equity you'd build if you stayed put.</p>

            <div class="callout" style="background: var(--bg-warm); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 18px 22px; margin: 20px 0;">
              <p style="margin: 0 0 10px; font-weight: 600; font-size: 16px; color: var(--ink);">→ Open the Rent vs Buy Calculator</p>
              <p style="margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 14px;">Enter your purchase price, down payment, current rent, and how long you plan to stay. See both scenarios side-by-side with a verdict on which one wins over your holding period.</p>
              <p style="margin: 0;"><a href="/rent-vs-buy-calculator" class="btn-primary" style="display: inline-block; padding: 8px 16px; font-size: 14px; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 4px;">Try it now →</a></p>
            </div>

            <p>Play with the "Years before selling" input. Watch how the numbers flip. That's the single biggest variable — how long you'll actually stay in the place.</p>

            <details class="b60-section-toggle" id="tips">
              <summary class="b60-section-toggle-summary">Tips before you commit</summary>
              <div class="b60-section-body">

            <ul class="bullets">
              <li><strong>Stress-test the interest rate.</strong> If today's rate is 4.5% but your renewal in 5 years is 6%, does the math still work? Try it in the calculator. If you'd be in trouble at 6%, don't lock in at 4.5%.</li>
              <li><strong>Don't drain your savings for the down payment.</strong> Keep at least 3–6 months of expenses liquid after you close. Furniture, appliances, moving, and the first repair bill will eat your cushion fast.</li>
              <li><strong>Get contents insurance on day one.</strong> Even if you're renting, contents insurance is $20–$40/month and covers your stuff, your liability, and a place to stay if the unit is uninhabitable. Landlord's policy covers zero of that. See my full <a href="/for-renters#insurance">insurance suggestions</a>.</li>
              <li><strong>Compare mortgage brokers.</strong> Different lenders quote different rates for the same borrower. A broker shops all of them at once — one hour of your time, potentially thousands in savings over five years.</li>
              <li><strong>Get the home inspection.</strong> $500–$700 that can save you $50,000. If a seller refuses to allow one, walk. Full stop.</li>
              <li><strong>Read the condo status certificate carefully.</strong> If you're buying a condo, your lawyer needs to review the status certificate. Look for special assessments, reserve fund health, and any legal actions against the corporation.</li>
              <li><strong>Talk to a mortgage broker AND a financial planner.</strong> The broker tells you what you can borrow. The planner tells you what you <em>should</em> borrow given the rest of your financial life.</li>
            </ul>
              </div>
            </details>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes I see</h2>
            <ul class="bullets">
              <li><strong>Buying because "everyone says I should."</strong> Family pressure is not a financial plan. Run the numbers for your situation.</li>
              <li><strong>Forgetting closing costs.</strong> Every first-time buyer forgets. Then they're scrambling for $20K in the last week before closing.</li>
              <li><strong>Buying at the top of what you can afford.</strong> The bank might approve you for $700K. That doesn't mean you should spend $700K. Leave yourself room to live.</li>
              <li><strong>Skipping the inspection to win a bidding war.</strong> A bad roof or foundation problem costs more than the house you lost by insisting on an inspection clause.</li>
              <li><strong>Comparing only the monthly payment.</strong> Rent is $2,500. Mortgage is $2,700. "See — buying is only $200 more!" No — buying is also $20K closing costs, $4K/year in property tax, $1K/year in insurance, and $6K/year in maintenance. Compare the FULL picture, not just the mortgage line.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>Bottom line: neither renting nor buying is universally right. It depends on your timeline, your income, your savings, and honestly — what kind of life you want. Play with the calculator. Talk to a broker. If it helps to talk it through with someone who's not selling you anything, that's what I'm here for.</p>

          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <CTASection heading="Thinking about buying your first place?" lead="Numbers on a page can only take you so far. If you want to run rent-vs-buy on real Toronto listings — with your actual down payment, rate, and holding period — send me your situation and let's talk it through. No pressure, no pitch." />
  <Footer />

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
</BaseLayout>
